1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,199
For five months, a dead dog kept circling the Earth, sealed alone inside a metal ball.

2
00:00:04,199 --> 00:00:07,099
Her name was Laika, and she was never meant to come home.

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:12,761
She was the first living thing ever to reach orbit, and the first to die there.

4
00:00:12,761 --> 00:00:18,421
The Soviet Union called her a hero, then lied about how she really died for the next forty five years.

5
00:00:19,566 --> 00:00:21,463
But Laika was only the beginning.

6
00:00:21,463 --> 00:00:26,836
To find out whether space would kill a human, we sent the animals up first. Fruit flies.

7
00:00:26,836 --> 00:00:29,048
Monkeys. Chimpanzees. A cat. And two tortoises.

8
00:00:30,166 --> 00:00:36,718
Some came home to live for decades. Some were lost at sea, or left in orbit on purpose.

9
00:00:36,718 --> 00:00:41,085
Every one of them paid for a piece of what we know.

10
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:49,312
This is every animal we sent into space, in order, from the very first passengers, to the two that beat every human being to the Moon.

11
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:51,844
The first passengers.

12
00:00:52,966 --> 00:00:58,929
February 1947. A captured German V-2 rocket lifts off from the New Mexico desert.

13
00:00:58,929 --> 00:01:02,762
Tucked inside is a small container of fruit flies.

14
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:09,297
They cross the hundred kilometer line into space, and their capsule parachutes back down.

15
00:01:09,297 --> 00:01:13,539
The flies are recovered alive. The first animals in space survived.

16
00:01:14,666 --> 00:01:17,006
The good luck did not last.

17
00:01:17,006 --> 00:01:25,977
Two years later, a rhesus monkey named Albert the Second becomes the first mammal in space, riding another V-2 above a hundred kilometers.

18
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:32,904
His heartbeat is recorded all the way up, and all the way down. Then his parachute fails.

19
00:01:32,904 --> 00:01:36,661
Albert the Second is killed on impact with the desert floor.

20
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:39,367
The dog they left in orbit.

21
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:45,580
November 1957. The Soviet Union has already shocked the world with Sputnik.

22
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:50,236
Now, to celebrate the revolution's anniversary, they rush a second satellite.

23
00:01:50,236 --> 00:01:52,777
This one will carry a passenger.

24
00:01:53,900 --> 00:02:02,233
She is a stray, plucked off the streets of Moscow days earlier, chosen because a stray had already survived hunger and cold.

25
00:02:02,233 --> 00:02:04,506
They name her Laika. Little Barker.

26
00:02:05,633 --> 00:02:11,778
Here is the part that still unsettles people. Sputnik 2 has no way to come back.

27
00:02:11,778 --> 00:02:15,235
There is no reentry capsule, no parachute, no plan.

28
00:02:15,235 --> 00:02:19,844
From the moment she is sealed in, Laika's flight is one way.

29
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:22,214
The engineers know it.

30
00:02:22,214 --> 00:02:30,640
One of them, years later, admits he took her home to play with his children before the launch, because he wanted to do something kind for her.

31
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:32,512
There was so little time left.

32
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:37,263
She launches strapped in a padded cabin, wired with sensors.

33
00:02:37,263 --> 00:02:43,796
Her heart races to triple its normal rate during the climb, then slowly settles as she reaches orbit.

34
00:02:43,796 --> 00:02:47,426
Laika becomes the first living being to circle the Earth.

35
00:02:48,566 --> 00:02:51,536
And then the cabin begins to overheat.

36
00:02:51,536 --> 00:02:56,627
A stage failed to separate cleanly, and the thermal control breaks down.

37
00:02:56,627 --> 00:02:59,172
The temperature climbs past forty degrees.

38
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:05,616
Laika died within about five to seven hours of launch, from overheating and stress.

39
00:03:05,616 --> 00:03:10,174
Not the peaceful several days the Soviet Union would describe for decades.

40
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:18,820
Her satellite kept circling for five more months with her body inside, before burning up on reentry in April 1958.

41
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:22,956
The world was told she had been humanely put to sleep.

42
00:03:22,956 --> 00:03:25,588
The real story stayed secret until 2002.

43
00:03:26,733 --> 00:03:31,033
One of the scientists who prepared her later said the quiet part out loud.

44
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:35,641
We did not learn enough from this mission to justify the death of the dog.

45
00:03:36,766 --> 00:03:38,177
The one lost at sea.

46
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,958
December 1958. The Americans are racing to catch up.

47
00:03:42,958 --> 00:03:49,869
They load a one pound squirrel monkey named Gordo into the nose cone of a Jupiter rocket.

48
00:03:49,869 --> 00:03:52,309
His handlers call him Old Reliable.

49
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:55,484
The flight goes almost perfectly.

50
00:03:55,484 --> 00:03:59,996
Gordo endures the launch, the weightlessness, the fall back to Earth.

51
00:03:59,996 --> 00:04:04,509
His heartbeat and breathing come through clean the entire way down.

52
00:04:05,633 --> 00:04:10,477
Then the nose cone hits the Atlantic, and the flotation device fails.

53
00:04:10,477 --> 00:04:16,531
The capsule, with Gordo inside, sinks into the ocean. Navy ships search for six hours.

54
00:04:16,531 --> 00:04:18,146
It is never found.

55
00:04:19,266 --> 00:04:21,369
He had done everything asked of him.

56
00:04:21,369 --> 00:04:25,875
He died because of a part the size of a fist that did not inflate.

57
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,228
The two who came back.

58
00:04:29,366 --> 00:04:33,110
Six months later, the Americans try again with two monkeys.

59
00:04:33,110 --> 00:04:40,599
Able, a rhesus, and Baker, a squirrel monkey small enough to fit in a capsule the size of a thermos.

60
00:04:41,733 --> 00:04:47,673
They fly, they endure the g forces, and they splash down. Both are recovered alive.

61
00:04:47,673 --> 00:04:50,842
The first American animals brought home from space.

62
00:04:50,842 --> 00:04:54,010
Their photos run in newspapers around the world.

63
00:04:55,133 --> 00:04:59,957
Four days later, Able dies, not from space, but on an operating table.

64
00:04:59,957 --> 00:05:05,895
During a routine surgery to remove an electrode, her heart fails in reaction to the anesthesia.

65
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:10,693
Baker, the tiny one, lived another twenty five years.

66
00:05:10,693 --> 00:05:16,792
She died in 1984 at Alabama's space museum, where children visited her by the thousands.

67
00:05:16,792 --> 00:05:19,232
She is buried on the grounds.

68
00:05:20,366 --> 00:05:22,952
The two who orbited, and the puppies.

69
00:05:24,099 --> 00:05:30,473
August 1960. The Soviets are ready to prove an animal can orbit the Earth and survive.

70
00:05:30,473 --> 00:05:35,253
They send up two dogs, Belka and Strelka. Squirrel and Little Arrow.

71
00:05:36,399 --> 00:05:42,470
They orbit the planet seventeen times, riding with a rabbit, mice, flies and plants.

72
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:49,408
After a full day in space, their capsule lands, and every creature aboard is recovered alive.

73
00:05:50,533 --> 00:05:54,670
It is the moment that proves a human could survive the same trip.

74
00:05:54,670 --> 00:05:57,534
Yuri Gagarin will fly less than a year later.

75
00:05:58,666 --> 00:06:03,511
And there is a strange, warm footnote. Strelka later had six puppies.

76
00:06:03,511 --> 00:06:11,584
One of them, a fluffy dog named Pushinka, was given as a gift to the family of President John F.

77
00:06:11,584 --> 00:06:11,988
Kennedy.

78
00:06:13,133 --> 00:06:17,333
Pushinka went on to have puppies of her own with the Kennedys' dog.

79
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:19,594
The President jokingly called them the pupniks.

80
00:06:19,594 --> 00:06:25,410
At the coldest point of the Cold War, two space dogs quietly linked Moscow and the White House.

81
00:06:26,533 --> 00:06:28,649
The chimps who did the job under fire.

82
00:06:29,766 --> 00:06:30,512
January 1961.

83
00:06:30,512 --> 00:06:35,731
Weeks before America dares to launch a person, they send a chimpanzee named Ham.

84
00:06:35,731 --> 00:06:43,559
He has been trained to pull levers when he sees a light, to prove an astronaut could still work in space.

85
00:06:44,699 --> 00:06:47,125
The flight goes wrong in the air.

86
00:06:47,125 --> 00:06:54,054
The rocket pushes too hard, the cabin loses pressure, and Ham is thrown through far higher g forces than planned.

87
00:06:54,054 --> 00:06:57,865
And through all of it, he keeps pulling his levers, correctly.

88
00:06:58,999 --> 00:07:05,994
He splashes down, is hauled aboard a recovery ship, and is famously photographed being offered an apple.

89
00:07:05,994 --> 00:07:12,165
Ham survived, and cleared the way for Alan Shepard weeks later. He lived to 1983.

90
00:07:13,299 --> 00:07:17,613
Later that year comes Enos, the only chimp to orbit the Earth.

91
00:07:17,613 --> 00:07:20,848
And his flight carried a cruelty no one intended.

92
00:07:21,966 --> 00:07:24,202
A malfunction reversed his training panel.

93
00:07:24,202 --> 00:07:31,282
Now, every time Enos pulled the correct lever, the machine gave him an electric shock instead of a reward.

94
00:07:31,282 --> 00:07:34,635
Seventy six times, he was punished for being right.

95
00:07:35,766 --> 00:07:40,353
And seventy six times, Enos kept pulling the correct lever anyway.

96
00:07:40,353 --> 00:07:46,607
He orbited twice, came home safely, and died of an unrelated illness the following year.

97
00:07:47,733 --> 00:07:48,882
The only cat.

98
00:07:49,999 --> 00:07:50,920
October 1963.

99
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:58,280
France enters the story with the one animal no other space program ever flew. A cat.

100
00:07:59,399 --> 00:08:08,677
She was a former Paris street cat, chosen from fourteen trained candidates, with electrodes implanted in her skull to send back signals from her brain.

101
00:08:08,677 --> 00:08:10,161
Her name was Félicette.

102
00:08:11,299 --> 00:08:15,535
A French rocket carried her past a hundred and fifty kilometers and back.

103
00:08:15,535 --> 00:08:19,119
Thirteen minutes after launch, she was on the ground again, alive.

104
00:08:19,119 --> 00:08:23,028
The only cat ever to go to space, and she survived it.

105
00:08:24,166 --> 00:08:26,938
And here the story turns cold again.

106
00:08:26,938 --> 00:08:32,879
Two to three months later, Félicette was put down so scientists could study her brain.

107
00:08:32,879 --> 00:08:36,443
The autopsy, by most accounts, revealed almost nothing useful.

108
00:08:37,566 --> 00:08:42,607
For decades she was almost forgotten, and often misremembered as a male cat named Félix.

109
00:08:42,607 --> 00:08:50,000
Only in recent years did she finally get a statue, a small bronze cat perched on top of the Earth, looking up.

110
00:08:51,133 --> 00:08:52,935
The two who beat us to the Moon.

111
00:08:54,066 --> 00:08:54,848
September 1968.

112
00:08:54,848 --> 00:09:03,840
Three months before Apollo 8 carries humans around the Moon, the Soviet Union quietly sends a spacecraft on a loop around it first.

113
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:05,795
On board are two tortoises.

114
00:09:06,933 --> 00:09:14,386
They fly around the far side of the Moon, further from Earth than any living thing has ever been, and swing back.

115
00:09:14,386 --> 00:09:17,774
Their capsule splashes into the Indian Ocean and is recovered.

116
00:09:18,899 --> 00:09:20,175
The tortoises are alive.

117
00:09:20,175 --> 00:09:28,148
They have lost about a tenth of their body weight, but they are active, and according to the report, they had not lost their appetite.

118
00:09:28,148 --> 00:09:32,614
The first Earthlings to circle the Moon were two tortoises, and they were hungry.

119
00:09:33,733 --> 00:09:38,675
Add them up. Fruit flies and dogs and monkeys and chimps, a cat, and two tortoises.

120
00:09:38,675 --> 00:09:46,089
Most of the story of getting humans safely into space was written first in the bodies of animals who had no say in it.

121
00:09:47,233 --> 00:09:52,526
Some, like Baker and Ham, lived long lives and were mourned by thousands.

122
00:09:52,526 --> 00:09:59,040
Others, like Laika and Gordo and Félicette, were never meant to survive the experiment at all.

123
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:08,227
Today there is a monument in Moscow with Laika standing on a rocket, and a bronze cat in France looking up at the sky.

124
00:10:08,227 --> 00:10:09,570
Small apologies, decades late.

125
00:10:10,699 --> 00:10:18,724
Every astronaut who ever came home safely owes a piece of that safety to an animal who went up first to find out what would happen.

126
00:10:18,724 --> 00:10:24,279
If this story stayed with you, the deaths of the humans who followed them is on screen now.

127
00:10:24,279 --> 00:10:27,365
And subscribe to mindwired, so the next story finds you.

